 "findings": [
  "Arm A (gsplat default 30k): with the room call's Spark settings the browser loses 0.50 dB PSNR (27.03 vs 27.53), SSIM -0.008, LPIPS +0.024. Matching Spark to the trainer recovers all but 0.12 dB.",
  "Arm A split: maxStdDev sqrt(5)->3 +0.09 dB; preBlur 0.3 without opacity compensation (A trained without --antialiased; Spark's default blurAmount compensates) +0.06; lifting maxPixelRadius 512, minAlpha 1/255, near 0.01, depth sort +0.22; float32 centres (extSplats) +0.01 PSNR, +0.001 SSIM.",
  "Arm B (our room settings): the call settings lose 2.21 dB (B_5000) and 3.98 dB (B_1250). Almost all of it is Spark's maxPixelRadius 512 cap: lifting only that takes B_5000 from -2.21 to -0.30 dB. B's MCMC splats were never densified past the 1.29M tie-point seeds, so near the camera they are large and get clipped, leaving holes (coverage 0.85 / 0.76).",
  "B_1250 keeps -1.58 dB even matched: its test views sit inside huge near-camera 'fog' splats whose centres are off screen; gsplat renders them with a clamped EWA Jacobian, Spark culls them (clipXY 1.4). Raising clipXY makes it worse: Spark does not clamp the Jacobian, so such splats blow up (A, 7 views: clipXY 100 -> 15.6 dB).",
  "SH1 export (the deployed default) costs only 0.05-0.11 dB more than SH3 for B.",
  "Sort timing: the pipeline/evaluate render page reads a view after 6 frames; for a 1.7M-splat room that can be before Spark's sort for the new view is back (A view 40: chair missing, 24.32 vs 28.02 dB settled). In SwiftShader (the software path every earlier score used) views 20 and 40 stayed stale even after 70 s of drawing; views 0 and 60 matched Metal to 0.01 dB. So SwiftShader-based scores of big rooms may carry stale-sort errors.",
  "Bottom line: for a properly trained splat (A) the browser path costs about 0.5 dB as deployed, 0.1 dB at best; for our room-preset splats (B) the deployed Spark settings cost 2-4 dB, mostly the 512 px splat radius cap."
 ],
